WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.410
Smart pointers are typed ownership tools.

2
00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:05.673
They help Rust programs represent heap
allocation, shared ownership,

3
00:00:05.753 --> 00:00:08.883
non-owning references, controlled
mutation, thread-safe sharing,

4
00:00:08.963 --> 00:00:11.934
stable-location assumptions, and flexible
borrow-or-own APIs.

5
00:00:12.014 --> 00:00:14.242
Their value comes from making ownership
visible,

6
00:00:14.322 --> 00:00:16.020
not from making ownership disappear.

7
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.169
Start with ordinary ownership and
borrowing.

8
00:00:18.249 --> 00:00:19.893
If owned heap indirection is needed,

9
00:00:19.973 --> 00:00:22.308
consider `Box<T>` before adding shared
ownership.

10
00:00:22.388 --> 00:00:26.154
If several parts of a single-threaded
design truly need ownership, `Rc<T>` may fit.

11
00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:28.887
If ownership must cross threads or tasks,
`Arc<T>` may fit.

12
00:00:28.967 --> 00:00:31.885
If a relationship should not keep the
value alive, use `Weak<T>`.

13
00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:35.148
Interior mutability and synchronization
deserve special attention.

14
00:00:35.228 --> 00:00:38.995
`Cell<T>`, `RefCell<T>`, `Mutex<T>`, and
`RwLock<T>` can support valid designs,

15
00:00:39.075 --> 00:00:42.735
but they change where access rules are
enforced and what runtime behaviors must be

16
00:00:42.815 --> 00:00:43.293
reviewed.

17
00:00:43.373 --> 00:00:46.874
Keep mutation scope small, lock scope
narrow, and failure behavior explicit.

18
00:00:46.954 --> 00:00:50.668
For async and concurrent systems, smart
pointers are not a complete safety design.

19
00:00:50.748 --> 00:00:54.462
Review shutdown, cancellation,
backpressure, resource limits, observability,

20
00:00:54.542 --> 00:00:55.550
and cleanup behavior.

21
00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:58.547
Shared ownership should not become a place
where tasks, handles,

22
00:00:58.627 --> 00:01:02.129
and resources live indefinitely because no
one knows who should release them.

23
00:01:02.209 --> 00:01:04.968
The practical checklist is to keep
ownership choices simple,

24
00:01:05.048 --> 00:01:08.231
document lifecycle assumptions, avoid
unnecessary shared mutation,

25
00:01:08.311 --> 00:01:11.865
use weak relationships for observers or
back-references, test cleanup paths,

26
00:01:11.945 --> 00:01:15.659
and review smart pointer use whenever it
affects resource lifetime, concurrency,

27
00:01:15.739 --> 00:01:18.020
API contracts, or long-running service
behavior.
